        "Qualitative tools reach what questionnaires miss. Interviews may be structured, semi-structured or in-depth, allowing probes that follow the respondent's own logic; focus groups of six to ten participants surface shared norms and disagreements; observation ranges from outsider note-taking to full participant immersion. Recording, verbatim transcription and coding turn talk into analysable text. Triangulating one survey finding with two interview themes strengthens a {S} conclusion far more than a bigger sample would.",
        "Reliability is consistency: a measure repeated under the same conditions should give the same result, tested by test-retest or split-half methods. Validity is truthfulness: the instrument must measure the concept it names, not a neighbouring one; face, content, criterion and construct validity are the usual checks. A scale that always reads three kilograms high is reliable but invalid. {S} reports must state both, since a valid but unstable index is as useless as a consistent wrong one.",
