        "Max Weber classified legitimate authority into three ideal types. Traditional authority rests on custom and heredity, as with emirs and obas; charismatic authority rests on the extraordinary personal appeal of a leader, unstable and typically routinised after death; legal-rational authority rests on impersonal rules and offices, the basis of modern bureaucracies. Real regimes blend types. {S} uses Weber's scheme to analyse why obeying an officeholder differs from obeying a person, and why elections change rulers without changing the state.",
